Openshift installation methods 

1. OpenShift Local (CRC – CodeReady Containers)
Purpose:
Local development and learning
What it is:
A single-node OpenShift cluster running on a laptop or desktop using a VM.
Key characteristics:
	• Runs on Linux, macOS, Windows
	• Uses libvirt, Hyper‑V, or VirtualBox
	• Includes OpenShift, Kubernetes, and core operators
	• Not for production use
	Use cases:
		○ Application development
		○ Operator development
		○ Feature exploration and demos
	Pros:
		○ Very easy to install
		○ Minimal setup
		○ Closest local experience to real OpenShift
		Cons:
			§ Single node only
			§ Limited resources
			§ No HA or production features
		
2. Installer-Provisioned Infrastructure (IPI)
Purpose:
Production-grade, automated cluster installation
What it is:
The recommended and most commonly used OpenShift installation method.
The OpenShift installer creates and manages infrastructure automatically.
Supported platforms:
	• AWS, Azure, GCP
	• VMware vSphere
	• OpenStack
	• Bare metal (with platform integrations)
	How it works:
		1. You provide a few inputs (platform, credentials, cluster size).
		2. The installer: 
			§ Creates VMs or bare‑metal resources
			§ Sets up networking, load balancers, DNS
			§ Installs control plane and worker nodes
			§ Configures etcd, operators, and cluster services
		Pros:
			§ Highly automated
			§ Fast and repeatable
			§ Supported upgrades and lifecycle management
			§ Best for enterprises
		Cons:
			§ Less control over low‑level infrastructure
			§ Cloud permissions required

✅ Best choice for most production environments

3. User-Provisioned Infrastructure (UPI)
Purpose:
Maximum infrastructure control
What it is:
You manually create the infrastructure, and OpenShift only installs the software.
You manage:
	• VMs or physical servers
	• Load balancers
	• DNS
	• Networking
	• Storage
OpenShift handles:
	• Kubernetes and OpenShift components
	• Cluster operators
	• Control plane software
	Common platforms:
		○ Bare metal
		○ VMware vSphere
		○ Restricted or disconnected environments
		○ Custom datacenters
		Pros:
			§ Full control over infrastructure
			§ Works in restricted or regulated environments
			§ No cloud automation dependency
			Cons:
				□ More complex
				□ Higher operational overhead
				□ Requires strong infrastructure expertise
				✅ Best for regulated, air‑gapped, or highly customized environments


4. Assisted Installer (Bare Metal & Edge)
Purpose:
Simplified bare-metal installation
What it is:
A guided OpenShift installer using a web UI or API, often used for:
	• Bare metal
	• Edge deployments
	• Small clusters
	How it works:
		○ You register hosts using an agent ISO
		○ Assisted Installer validates hardware and networking
		○ Automates most of the UPI complexity
		○ Can run hosted (Red Hat SaaS) or self-managed
		Pros:
			§ Easier than full UPI
			§ Hardware validation included
			§ Great for edge and telco use cases
			Cons:
				□ Less automated than IPI
				□ Limited customization compared to full UPI
			
5. OpenShift on Bare Metal
Purpose:
High performance, low latency, on‑prem deployments
Installation styles:
	• IPI on bare metal
	• UPI on bare metal
	• Assisted Installer
	Key features:
		○ No virtualization overhead
		○ Full hardware access
		○ Common in telecom, finance, and manufacturing
		Challenges:
			§ Networking and load balancer setup
			§ Requires careful planning



6. Managed OpenShift Services (Hosted Installation)
Purpose:
Remove cluster management burden
What it is:
Red Hat (or cloud providers) install, manage, and operate OpenShift for you.
Examples:
	• ROSA (Red Hat OpenShift Service on AWS)
	• ARO (Azure Red Hat OpenShift)
	• OpenShift on IBM Cloud
	Responsibilities:
		○ Platform provider manages: 
			§ Control plane
			§ Upgrades
			§ Infrastructure
			§ You manage: 
				□ Applications
				□ Users
				□ Namespaces
				Pros:
					® Least operational overhead
					® SLA-backed
					® Fast time to value
					Cons:
						◊ Less customization
						◊ Cloud lock‑in
						◊ Cost considerations
						✅ Best for teams focused on applications, not platform ops
						
						
7. Disconnected (Air‑Gapped) Installation
Purpose:
Secure environments with no internet access
How it works:
	• Uses either IPI or UPI
	• Requires: 
		○ Local container registry
		○ Mirrored OpenShift images
		○ Offline operator catalogs
		Common in:
			§ Government
			§ Defense
			§ Regulated industries
			Complexity: High
			Control: Very high






